      },
      "text": "```\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                  COMICS HISTORY TIMELINE                         |\n|                                                                  |\n|  PRE-HISTORY                                                     |\n|  Bayeux Tapestry (1070s): sequential narrative in image          |\n|  Hogarth: \"A Rake's Progress\" (1735): sequential moral satire    |\n|  Rodolphe Topffer (1827-1845): first proto-comics albums         |\n|                                                                  |\n|  NEWSPAPER COMICS (1890s-present)                                |\n|  1895: Yellow Kid (Outcault) -- first widely syndicated strip    |\n|  1905: Little Nemo (McCay) -- visual imagination                 |\n|  1913: Krazy Kat (Herriman) -- language and surrealism           |\n|  1929: Tintin (Herge) -- BD European tradition begins            |\n|                                                                  |\n|  COMIC BOOKS (1930s-present)                                     |\n|  1938: Action Comics #1 (Superman) -- superhero era begins       |\n|  1954: Comics Code Authority -- censorship and suppression       |\n|  1968: Zap Comix -- underground comix; R. Crumb                  |\n|  1978: A Contract with God (Eisner) -- first \"graphic novel\"     |\n|  1986-92: Literary maturity: Watchmen, Dark Knight, Maus         |\n|                                                                  |\n|  GLOBAL EXPANSION (1980s-present)                                |\n|  Manga globalization; BD as art form; webcomics                  |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```"
    },
